  <!-- STATUS: AUDITED -->
  <preface xml:id="preface">
    <title>Preface</title>
    <p>Calculus connects local change, accumulated change, and approximation. This book begins with velocity and distance, develops derivatives and integrals together with their hypotheses, and uses them to study models, numerical methods, differential equations, and series.</p><p>The prerequisites are algebra, coordinate geometry, and trigonometry in radians. Appendices A–C review those tools. Worked examples, conceptual checkpoints, warning examples, and projects keep calculation, explanation, and application in contact.</p>
  </preface>

  <!-- STATUS: AUDITED -->
  <preface xml:id="how-to-read">
    <title>How to Read This Book</title>
    <p>Read the chapter introductions for the guiding questions. Work through each example with pencil and paper before reading its calculation, then answer the checkpoints in your own words. A formula should be read together with its domain and hypotheses.</p><p>Use the warning examples to test the limits of a rule. Use computing sections and projects to compare exact reasoning with numerical evidence; report an error bound whenever an approximation is used. The later previews show where single-variable calculus leads, and may be read after the core derivative, integral, and series material.</p><p>Sections headed <em>Additional topics</em> are optional extensions. Richardson and Romberg integration and adaptive quadrature follow numerical integration; Runge–Kutta methods follow differential equations; contraction iteration follows sequences; and the Weierstrass M-test follows uniform convergence. The local existence and uniqueness theorem is placed at the end of the book because its proof uses the Cauchy criterion, contraction theorem, and uniform convergence. It can be read after those topics without the intervening complex-number material.</p><p>Return to Appendices A–C for prerequisite review, Appendix D for proof details, Appendix E for technology notes, and Appendix F for formula and test summaries.</p>
  </preface>
</frontmatter>
